---
// Task 6, Fix round 3 — makes a clicked duplicate take row the View
// Transitions morph target, instead of always morphing from wherever the
// FIRST occurrence of that take id happens to sit on the page.
//
// Background: the same take can legitimately appear twice on one render
// (`/`: favorites + recent-events/needs-your-vote; `/me`: favorites +
// votes). A duplicate `view-transition-name` silently aborts the WHOLE
// PAGE's transition (CSS View Transitions L1), so round 1 made it
// first-occurrence-wins — see `TakeRow.astro`'s header comment for
// `transitionName`. That's correct but means clicking the SECOND
// occurrence still morphs from the FIRST occurrence's on-screen position.
// This component fixes that by moving the `take-<id>` name onto whichever
// occurrence was actually clicked, before Astro captures the "old" page.
//
// Import this ONLY on pages that can render the same take twice in one
// render (`/` and `/me` today) — never from `AppLayout.astro` itself, so
// `/songs`, `/events`, `/search`, and `/takes/[id]` (none of which can
// duplicate a take) ship no extra client JS. Every element this script
// touches must already carry `data-take-id` — see `TakeRow.astro` and
// `me/index.astro`'s votes-list link.
//
// Progressive enhancement, not enablement: with JS off, or when
// `prefers-reduced-motion` is set (Astro itself then skips the visual
// transition — see `AppLayout.astro`'s `<ClientRouter />` comment), this
// script does nothing observable, and round 1's first-occurrence-wins
// naming is exactly what still happens. Navigation itself never depends on
// this running — every take link is a plain `<a href>`.
---

<script>
  // Delegated on `document`, and every element re-queried by attribute at
  // the moment it's needed — never a reference held across a swap. Astro
  // replaces <body> wholesale on navigation, AND this exact script text is
  // imported on more than one page (`/` and `/me`): Astro dedupes <script>
  // tags by textContent and will NOT re-run this on a same-tab navigation
  // between two pages that both include it (see AppLayout.astro's
  // route-progress script for the same lesson, learned earlier in this
  // project) — so a reference captured once would go stale exactly the way
  // that script's own header comment warns about.
  //
  // Hook point — verified empirically against the built server (see
  // task-6-report.md, "Fix round 3"): the retarget has to happen in a
  // CAPTURING-phase click listener, not (as a first attempt assumed)
  // `astro:before-preparation`. Instrumenting both with logging showed
  // `astro:before-preparation` already firing — synchronously, before any
  // bubble-phase `click` listener on `document` gets a turn — because
  // `<ClientRouter />`'s own click listener is registered (bubble phase,
  // no capture) before this script ever runs, and it fires
  // `astro:before-preparation` itself as part of handling the click. A
  // bubble-phase listener here would always observe it having already
  // happened. The third `true` argument below (`capture: true`) is load-
  // bearing: it makes this listener run during the CAPTURING phase, which
  // completes before the event even reaches its target element, and so
  // strictly before ClientRouter's bubble-phase listener — regardless of
  // which script tag happens to run first. That is also why the retarget
  // is done directly in the click handler now, with no second, separate
  // `astro:before-preparation` step: by the time ClientRouter (and
  // anything it triggers) sees this click at all, the name has already
  // moved.
  document.addEventListener(
    "click",
    (event) => {
      // Astro itself skips the visual transition under reduced motion (its
      // own built-in behavior — see AppLayout.astro's comment on
      // `<ClientRouter />`); retargeting a name nobody will see morph is
      // unobservable work this script shouldn't bother doing.
      if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) {
        return;
      }

      const target = event.target;
      const link = target instanceof Element ? target.closest("[data-take-id]") : null;
      if (!(link instanceof HTMLElement)) {
        return;
      }
      const takeId = link.dataset.takeId;
      if (!takeId) {
        return;
      }

      const name = `take-${takeId}`;
      const matches = document.querySelectorAll(`[data-take-id="${CSS.escape(takeId)}"]`);
      for (const el of matches) {
        if (!(el instanceof HTMLElement)) {
          continue;
        }
        // Every take-row link already carries its own unique
        // `data-astro-transition-scope` (Astro's compiler adds one to
        // every element using the `transition:name` directive, regardless
        // of the value it resolves to). Reusing it as the "off" name for
        // every OTHER occurrence is exactly the harmless, unique fallback
        // Astro would already have assigned that element — so un-naming
        // it here can never reintroduce a duplicate `view-transition-name`.
        el.style.viewTransitionName = el === link ? name : (el.dataset.astroTransitionScope ?? "");
      }
    },
    true,
  );
</script>
